    "content": "JOWHAR — The water level of the Shabelle River in Jowhar has risen sharply, prompting flood alerts for the town, Balcad district, and farming villages along the riverbank. Data from FAO-SWALIM indicates that as of 14 October, the river stood at 5.38 metres — just 13 centimetres below its flood threshold.\n\nThe current rise follows Deyr rains and inflows from the Beledweyne and Buuloburde areas. Additional rainfall inside Somalia and in the Ethiopian highlands could further increase water volumes, according to the same monitoring data.\n\nRiverine communities in Middle Shabelle are particularly vulnerable to flooding. Many residents rely on farming and livestock, and displacement often follows when the river overflows. Local authorities have not yet issued evacuation orders, but residents are advised to remain vigilant.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, flooding in the Jowhar area carries direct implications. Many diaspora members have relatives in the region and contribute to family support and local development projects. Flooding can disrupt remittance flows, damage property, and displace loved ones, often leading to increased requests for assistance from abroad.\n\nThis is not the first time the Shabelle River has threatened the region. Similar alerts have been issued in past years, highlighting the need for sustained investment in flood defences and early warning systems. The diaspora has sometimes played a role in funding such measures through hometown associations and NGOs.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/16/wabiga-shabeelle-oo-qarka-u-saaran-inuu-ku-fataho-jowhar-iyo-deegaannada-ku-dhow/",
