    "id": "news-1792089975412-0",
    "title": "Forces Loyal to Former Southwest President Seize Burhakabo in Bay Region",
    "original_title": "Somalia&#8217;s government loses Burhakabo to ousted Southwest leader’s forces",
    "slug": "forces-loyal-to-former-southwest-president-seize-burhakabo-in-bay-region",
    "summary": "Forces loyal to former Southwest State president Abdiasis Laftagaren captured Burhakabo, a strategic town in Somalia's Bay region, on Thursday, marking another territorial setback for the federal government.",
    "content": "Forces loyal to Abdiasis Laftagaren, the former president of Somalia's Southwest State, took control of Burhakabo, a strategic town in the Bay region, on Thursday, according to local sources. The town, located approximately 64 kilometers east of Baidoa, lies along the main road connecting Mogadishu, the national capital, and Baidoa, the administrative center of the Bay region. The seizure followed a brief firefight early in the day, after which Laftagaren's forces secured full control of the area. It was not immediately clear whether the clash resulted in casualties, though unconfirmed reports suggested some injuries.\n\nThe capture of Burhakabo is the latest in a series of territorial gains by Laftagaren's forces in recent weeks, deepening the federal government's losses in the region. Laftagaren, who was ousted from the Southwest State presidency in 2023, has been engaged in a power struggle with federal authorities and the current regional administration. His forces have previously seized other towns in the Bay and Bakool regions, challenging the government's authority in Somalia's southwestern corridor.\n\nThe federal government has not yet issued a statement on the fall of Burhakabo. The town's location on the Mogadishu-Baidoa road makes it economically and strategically significant, as it serves as a key transit route for goods and people between the capital and the agricultural hub of Baidoa. Control of this road could disrupt trade and humanitarian access in a region already facing periodic insecurity.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the development raises concerns about stability in the Southwest State, which is home to many diaspora families and where remittances play a vital role in the local economy. Diaspora members often invest in businesses and properties in Baidoa and surrounding areas, and prolonged instability could jeopardize those investments and delay planned visits or family reunifications. Additionally, the ongoing power struggle may complicate efforts by international partners and diaspora organizations to support development and humanitarian projects in the region.\n\nThis event underscores the persistent fragility of Somalia's federal system, where regional and federal actors frequently clash over authority and resources. The Somali government, already battling al-Shabaab militants, now faces a renewed internal challenge that could divert attention and resources from counterinsurgency efforts. The international community, including diaspora-backed initiatives, has consistently called for dialogue to resolve such disputes peacefully, but the situation remains fluid.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-government-loses-burhakabo-southwest/",
    "source": "somaliguardian.com",
    "source_name": "Somalia Guardian",
    "original_url": "https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-government-loses-burhakabo-southwest/",
    "country": "Somalia",
